# dns1: /etc/hosts
#
# Before any DNS server is asked, most programs look in /etc/hosts (the
# order is set in /etc/nsswitch.conf, more on that in dns5). Format, one
# address per line:
#     ADDRESS   CANONICAL_NAME   [ALIASES...]
#
# The check copies this file to /etc/hosts inside the sandbox (the sandbox
# has its own private /etc, your real one is not touched), starts a web
# server on b (10.0.0.2), and then:
#     getent hosts api.internal   -> 10.0.0.2
#     getent hosts api            -> 10.0.0.2   (short alias)
#     getent hosts db.internal    -> 10.0.0.3
#     curl http://api.internal/   -> "hello from api"
# `getent hosts NAME` resolves a name exactly the way programs do (unlike
# dig, which only talks DNS).
#
# Your task: fix this file. Someone wrote one line the wrong way round and
# forgot the alias.

127.0.0.1     localhost
::1           localhost
api.internal  10.0.0.2
10.0.0.3      db.internal
